      {
        "name": "breeding",
        "start": "01-01",
        "end": "12-31",
        "note": "Breeding can occur year-round in mild climates; multiple litters."
      },
      {
        "name": "young",
        "start": "01-01",
        "end": "12-31",
        "note": "'Cottontails give birth to their kits in burrows vacated by other mammals. They sometimes cool off, or take refuge in scratched out shallow created depressions of their own making, using their front paws like a back hoe.' Survival to adulthood is the limiting factor: 'almost every local carnivore larger or faster than the lagomorph is its predator', and snakes 'can catch and eat the young at will; the mother is unable to defend the litter'."
      },
      {
        "name": "dormancy",
        "start": "11-01",
        "end": "02-28",
        "fraction": 0.5,
        "note": "'They are not usually active in the middle of the day, but can be observed foraging in the early morning, and early evening. Cottontails are rarely found out of their burrows looking for food on windy days, because the wind interferes with their ability to hear approaching predators.' The phase records the cool-season shelter in burrows and depressions; it is not hibernation, and the fraction is judgement."
      }
